Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></td><td style="text-align: justify;">The density of the nucleus is much higher than that of ordinary matter.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></td><td style="text-align: justify;"><span id="cke_bm_1110S" style="display: none;">&nbsp;</span>Most of the mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus while the size of this nucleus is almost <span class="math-tex">\(10^5\)</span> times smaller.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td style="width: 6%;">1.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td style="width: 6%;">2.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td style="width: 6%;">3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td style="width: 6%;">4.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are False.</td></tr></tbody></table>
